 "conventions": {
  "defaultForbid": "shoot, set_change, undo and who are forbidden in every entry unless its `expected` or `allow` names them. A `who` equal to the fixture's NOW who (after context) counts as absent: it is not a swap.",
  "forbid": "extra keys that must be absent.",
  "allow": "keys that may appear without being required; overrides defaultForbid for those keys.",
  "expected": "a scalar must be equal; a string array must include all its items; {anyOf:[...]} must equal or include one of them; {includes:[...]} and {excludes:[...]} are case-insensitive substrings (excludes passes when the key is absent); {min,max} is a number range; {minItems:n} is an array length; {candidates:[...]} is a thing list with exactly those aliases in any order; a top-level anyOf is a list of alternative expectation objects.",
  "direction": "the composed 'what happens' after the turn: composeHappens(reading.happens) against context.now.direction (or the fixture's).",
  "set_change": "checks set_change.said, which must also be an exact substring of the phrase; set_change_gloss checks the gloss.",
  "near.thing / gaze.thing / facing.thing": "an alias, or {candidates:[...]}.",
  "expectShoot": "none (default) | still | take: shootDecision(plan, state) for the entry's mode, with source 'message'.",
  "expectCards": "when present, exactly these Needs cards: astra | which | take | hour. Absent: not checked.",
  "expectReplyMentions": "case-insensitive substrings of the reply composed in context.locale (default en).",
  "context": "now overrides fixture NOW fields; turns is LAST TURNS; locale is the interface language; origin 'build' marks the Sets home's first message on a set it just built; named true reads the set as the naming pass would name it (lib/fixtures.mts HAND_NAMES), with its parts. Without it the set is read as saved, nameless (the fixture file itself), as every set is until an admin names it.",
  "aliases": "t1… are the set's things by their place in the set's own order, s1… its named parts by their place among the parts, p1… the characters. A set is read as saved, nameless, unless the phrase's context says named; then a thing's `name` and each part are what the reader is told, and only then may a phrase name an s-alias."
 },
